          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What downspout size should I use with a 6\" copper half-round gutter?",
            "icon": "check_mark",
            "row_content": "<p>The standard match is a <strong>3\" round downspout</strong>, which works well for most residential applications. For larger roof areas or heavy rainfall, consider upgrading to a <strong>4\" or even 5\" round downspout</strong> for increased drainage capacity. All options are compatible with our 6\" copper gutter system.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_row_Whet9w": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What’s the difference between 16 oz. and 20 oz. copper?",
            "icon": "check_mark",
            "row_content": "<p><strong>16 oz. copper</strong> is suitable for most homes and provides excellent durability. <strong>20 oz. copper</strong> is thicker and better suited for commercial buildings, slate roofs, or regions with heavy snow and ice.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_row_PxPiPk": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Will the copper change color over time?",
            "icon": "check_mark",
            "row_content": "<p>Yes. Copper will naturally age and develop a patina. It starts with a bright finish, then transitions to a darker bronze, and eventually to a greenish tone. This aging process protects the material and adds character over time.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_row_gfqPxj": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Do I need sealant for copper gutter joints?",
            "icon": "check_mark",
            "row_content": "<p>For best results, <strong>soldering</strong> is the recommended method for joining copper components. It creates a permanent, watertight bond. If soldering isn’t feasible, a high-quality metal gutter sealant can be used, but it may require future maintenance.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
